# Copy to .env.local for local development, and set the same values in
# Vercel > Settings > Environment Variables for deployments.
#
# Without RESEND_API_KEY the site still runs — the chat assistant answers from
# the demo knowledge base in src/data/chat.ts and captured leads are logged
# server-side. That is enough to demo the flow, but not to run on.

# ---- Lead email (REQUIRED before launch) ----------------------------
# Briefs from /contact, the chat widget and the newsletter signup are
# emailed to Formulyn through Resend. Get a key at resend.com/api-keys.
# RESEND_API_KEY=re_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

# Where briefs land. Defaults to site.email (info@formulyn.com.au).
# Comma-separate to notify more than one person.
# LEADS_EMAIL_TO=info@formulyn.com.au

# The sending address. Must be on a domain verified in Resend
# (Resend > Domains > add formulyn.com.au, then add the DNS records).
# Until that is done, leave this unset: Resend's shared onboarding@resend.dev
# sender is used, which only delivers to the Resend account owner's address.
# LEADS_EMAIL_FROM=Formulyn <briefs@formulyn.com.au>

# ---- Optional: also POST leads to a CRM or automation tool ----------
# Fire-and-forget, in addition to the email above. Safe to leave unset.
# LEADS_WEBHOOK_URL=https://hooks.example.com/formulyn-leads
# LEADS_WEBHOOK_SECRET=    # sent as: Authorization: Bearer <secret>

# ---- Chat assistant -------------------------------------------------
# Set CHAT_API_URL and the live provider replaces the demo answers.
#
# The bot is trained by the system prompt in src/data/chat-prompt.ts — that
# file is the FAQ knowledge base, so edit it to retrain the bot. It is POSTed
# on every turn both as a top-level "system" field and as a leading system
# message, so it applies whichever convention your API follows:
#
#   { "system": "...", "messages": [{ "role", "content" }, ...] }
#
# The reply is read from content / reply / message.content / content[].text —
# adjust src/lib/chat/live-provider.ts if your API differs.
# CHAT_API_URL=https://your-api.example.com/chat
# CHAT_API_KEY=            # sent as: Authorization: Bearer <key>

# How the system prompt is attached. Leave unset ("both") unless the API
# rejects one of the two forms — a strict Anthropic endpoint 400s on a
# role:"system" entry inside messages, so use "system" there.
#   both     (default) top-level "system" field AND a leading system message
#   system   top-level "system" field only        — Anthropic-style
#   message  leading system message only          — OpenAI-style
# If the bot answers off-script, it is almost always this setting: the API
# accepted the request but ignored the form the prompt arrived in.
# CHAT_API_SYSTEM_MODE=both

# ---- Google reviews sync (optional) ---------------------------------
# Set both and the homepage testimonials start syncing from the Google
# Business Profile listing: the score, the review count, and any review not
# already curated in src/data/home.ts. Leave either unset and the curated
# seven show on their own, exactly as before.
#
# Setup, once:
#   1. Google Cloud console > new project > enable "Places API (New)".
#      Billing must be on, but one fetch a day (~30 calls a month) is inside
#      the free allowance — expect a $0 bill.
#   2. Credentials > create an API key > restrict it to Places API (New).
#      Server-side only: do NOT rename this with a NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix.
#   3. Find the place ID with Google's Place ID Finder:
#      https://developers.google.com/maps/documentation/places/web-service/place-id
#
# Reviews refresh every 24 hours and are published unfiltered — a one-star
# review will appear on the homepage on its own. Vetting reviews before they
# go live needs a moderation step, which this does not have.
#
# Heads up: this endpoint returns AT MOST 5 reviews and Google picks which
# five. Publishing every review needs the Business Profile API, a different
# product with an OAuth flow and an access-request form.
# GOOGLE_PLACES_API_KEY=AIza...
# GOOGLE_PLACE_ID=ChIJ...
